    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-removal-of-conclusive-certificates-and-other-measures-act-2009-sch-1-sec-2009-2",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "2009 No. 99, 2009",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-removal-of-conclusive-certificates-and-other-measures-act-2009#sch-1-sec-2009-2"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "freedom-of-information-removal-of-conclusive-certificates-and-other-measures-act-2009.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "freedom-of-information-removal-of-conclusive-certificates-and-other-measures-act-2009.pdf, p.10",
      "values:originalText": "(4) For the purposes of determining, under subsection (3), whether it\nwould not be appropriate for the person or organisation, or the\nproprietor of the undertaking, to be informed under subsection (3),\nthe Tribunal must have regard to whether informing the person or\norganisation, or the proprietor of the undertaking, would, or could\nreasonably be expected to:\n\n(a) prejudice the conduct of an investigation of a breach, or\npossible breach, of the law, or a failure, or possible failure, to\ncomply with a law relating to taxation or prejudice the\nenforcement or proper administration of the law in a\nparticular instance; or\n\n(b) disclose, or enable a person to ascertain, the existence or\nidentity of a confidential source of information, or the\nnon-existence of a confidential source of information, in\nrelation to the enforcement or administration of the law; or\n\n(c) endanger the life or physical safety of any person; or\n\n(d) cause damage to the security, defence or international\nrelations of the Commonwealth.",
      "skos:definition": "(4) For the purposes of determining, under subsection (3), whether it\nwould not be appropriate for the person or organisation, or the\nproprietor of the undertaking, to be informed under subsection (3),\nthe Tribunal must have regard to whether informing the person or\norganisation, or the proprietor of the undertaking, would, or could\nreasonably be expected to:\n\n(a) prejudice the conduct of an investigation of a breach, or\npossible breach, of the law, or a failure, or possible failure, to\ncomply with a law relating to taxation or prejudice the\nenforcement or proper administration of the law in a\nparticular instance; or\n\n(b) disclose, or enable a person to ascertain, the existence or\nidentity of a confidential source of information, or the\nnon-existence of a confidential source of information, in\nrelation to the enforcement or administration of the law; or\n\n(c) endanger the life or physical safety of any person; or\n\n(d) cause damage to the security, defence or international\nrelations of the Commonwealth.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-removal-of-conclusive-certificates-and-other-measures-act-2009-sch-1-sec-2009-2-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:freedom-of-information-removal-of-conclusive-certificates-and-other-measures-act-2009-sch-1-sec-2009-2-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "bfb659b8a0b8dece80c439ae68a887f7cbb20fbc233355ac23c29e303c9df53e",
      "skos:note": "Informing a person or organisation about an investigation may prejudice the conduct of the investigation."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/freedom-of-information-removal-of-conclusive-certificates-and-other-measures-act-2009#sch-1-sec-2009-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "2009 No. 99, 2009",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 10,
      "values:originalText": "(4) For the purposes of determining, under subsection (3), whether it\nwould not be appropriate for the person or organisation, or the\nproprietor of the undertaking, to be informed under subsection (3),\nthe Tribunal must have regard to whether informing the person or\norganisation, or the proprietor of the undertaking, would, or could\nreasonably be expected to:\n\n(a) prejudice the conduct of an investigation of a breach, or\npossible breach, of the law, or a failure, or possible failure, to\ncomply with a law relating to taxation or prejudice the\nenforcement or proper administration of the law in a\nparticular instance; or\n\n(b) disclose, or enable a person to ascertain, the existence or\nidentity of a confidential source of information, or the\nnon-existence of a confidential source of information, in\nrelation to the enforcement or administration of the law; or\n\n(c) endanger the life or physical safety of any person; or\n\n(d) cause damage to the security, defence or international\nrelations of the Commonwealth."
    },
